e text; use idx from the message position: the message appears after prefix, and exception text after.\n```\nActually the original code does `prefix = msg[: msg.find(record.message)]`. So they assume find works. We can do: after super().format(record), find the message in msg and replace with truncated version:\n```\ntruncated = ...\nif truncated is not None:\n    idx = msg.find(full_message)\n    if idx >= 0:\n        msg = msg[:idx] + truncated + msg[idx+len(full_message):]\n```\nEdge: full_message might be empty → no truncation anyway.\n\nBut careful: if the message contains newlines and we need prefix alignment; existing code aligns after. That's fine.\n\nAlternatively, a cleaner way: build the record copy. Let's consider tests: test_message_budget_and_alignment with multiline; test checks output content presumably. Using replacement approach should be fine.\n\nHmm, but a subtlety: exception text appended after message — when truncating we keep the message body truncated and exception intact. With the replace approach, exception stays. Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: whats if record.message appears in the time prefix? e.g. message \"2024\". find gives the prefix occurrence, causing broken output. Safer: compute prefix length by formatting? We can get the prefix via msg.find(record.message) like original... same risk. Better approach: compute the head portion deterministically.\n\nBetter solution: override formatMessage? Actually we can compute the prefix ourselves:\nprefix = self._style.format(record) which produces the prefix + message using record.message. Then prefix_str = full_style[: len(full_style) - len(record.message)] if it endswith record.message. formatMessage returns self._style.format(record) i.e. prefix+message. So:\n\n```\ndef format(self, record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    truncated = self._apply_budget(record, message)\n    if truncated is not None:\n        # temporarily set record.message\n        original = record.message\n        re